#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208d1741-9b10-5838-b5d0-cf5b8af2cbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269544} {:answer/id #uuid "6e85d1f1-1d07-59dc-a62c-55b15d9603cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270250} {:answer/id #uuid "70ac0a62-886f-57c1-aea0-e66bd7901f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270268} {:answer/id #uuid "9754293a-effe-5066-a8b6-e33c8c81be55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270596} {:answer/id #uuid "dffd9eb1-554e-5064-8f37-aa14a874c3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e85d1f1-1d07-59dc-a62c-55b15d9603cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270250} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdc4b03-2ed5-57f1-96a3-caaa4804b205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fc1f275-1b92-5d39-91a9-c77fc478abd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141461" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302eb5c9-529d-52e1-a2c8-d1933af5ef1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269702} {:answer/id #uuid "3248b20a-91c4-5f7a-8779-ea6de359ff74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269724} {:answer/id #uuid "50abda73-b0d4-5a29-bbed-94088d819a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269993} {:answer/id #uuid "81cab381-305e-5227-96c9-4247414173f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270391} {:answer/id #uuid "aa256175-82e0-56ad-9450-2d16ed6ca6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "302eb5c9-529d-52e1-a2c8-d1933af5ef1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269702} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf48405-f1ff-5682-8eff-4a48eebe4c60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4927fd87-c83e-52d3-b9bb-877110a8c514" :question/math-academy-id "q-142474" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}?\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)<4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c41b95-9596-5622-8c50-ab125bfd2d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269450} {:answer/id #uuid "219e0837-40ab-56ac-83a2-e830963ec4b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269555} {:answer/id #uuid "31ab7072-4441-5672-aae6-91ba5c541bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269716} {:answer/id #uuid "36aebee8-74d8-57f5-bd26-af2dbc3f65cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269765} {:answer/id #uuid "a3580b5c-8adc-5496-932c-814534e0f45c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36aebee8-74d8-57f5-bd26-af2dbc3f65cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269765} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78fac35-412b-5106-bc11-6026065b218b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a4ded73-dd50-5431-9dd3-a7f4831d94c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-142544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix which has only $3$ non-pivot column?\n\n- $A$ is  **not** invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=7=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ has $n=7$ columns and $3$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=7-3=4\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n Thus,  according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{7}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $n=7$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e429d6-5947-5ad4-8a8e-1b4250c00d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273111} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3fa507-d42c-5103-9bce-8c5f53c501b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273180} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c0cc8f-fdd7-579a-8085-29cf09cbcaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274381} {:answer/id #uuid "f79576de-3c96-5d9f-9017-a24e98f90c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274559} {:answer/id #uuid "fe165258-8e9f-54a9-8b34-94b679b7bcec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe165258-8e9f-54a9-8b34-94b679b7bcec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274621} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b6204e6-2ff1-5255-b86a-87eaf2ede716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5342a760-4f6b-57f7-989c-35eadd9600b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143533" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}?$ \n\n- $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=3=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}=\\text{Span}{\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08aa5e40-f907-50f4-951b-c71140a5352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272462} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8077df-ab1d-5da4-88d8-80f362bafcad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272895} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e2eb0-f74e-552b-b366-11725709bbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273721} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf1cae4-760d-5c26-a55c-ccba7d6302c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273739} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e1a958-b3c4-543b-af2a-389cf376a09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e2eb0-f74e-552b-b366-11725709bbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273721} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ad96634-e2da-5afd-bd87-58989b7b67fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df88ef9d-a81d-55a1-b088-252f805046b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59511ecd-760f-5a26-8413-c806af70d849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273150} {:answer/id #uuid "74394f47-61a5-59ed-8042-940ea3fed5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273382} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b1f62c-834f-579c-a300-554956679735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273963} {:answer/id #uuid "c0528799-16a1-5629-96a3-8f50e1d8f001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274052} {:answer/id #uuid "dc006e14-42a2-5536-a14c-1676307fd9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74394f47-61a5-59ed-8042-940ea3fed5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273382} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8b66c2-34e1-5bb1-904f-4db1604f1532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "416a1d82-1004-5007-b882-19d5aaa3af4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143686" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A+{A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15274c66-a01d-5e11-adcf-f5b7af833716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272577} {:answer/id #uuid "33f93cc6-5c19-5d84-abe2-dc0f4f827464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272849} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfa656f-46fd-5b47-8681-a4125453c988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272905} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec937ca-a0b9-5506-bdd2-a01d7c5c3993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273474} {:answer/id #uuid "c17c205f-6fa2-54a3-af74-d30ca7ac801f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec937ca-a0b9-5506-bdd2-a01d7c5c3993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273474} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f76ddf6-2fd6-51e1-9516-877b87bf72c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62cdcd34-c338-59e0-b65d-2d882ff231e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143687" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048a1426-e467-5f7e-8f82-a4d34764959c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272427} {:answer/id #uuid "5277c838-1f6f-5a65-99df-70de2b3be811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273088} {:answer/id #uuid "91b72d80-d1b0-5714-90ef-ff9b08666590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273654} {:answer/id #uuid "96acc4c6-843e-5d92-95f4-295d7b6576f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273692} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb2b63c-1f8a-5d48-8978-0439e3ae672a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfb2b63c-1f8a-5d48-8978-0439e3ae672a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274196} :answer-field/id #uuid "efa0f764-9003-584a-9cb0-87716a826d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "812925c4-9b82-5457-b638-58a29d609de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143688" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31450770-cd81-593c-9756-bf59a3210e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272828} {:answer/id #uuid "4001b683-f061-56c7-b09f-ae4da2370e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272936} {:answer/id #uuid "748af4df-aea9-5cea-aff7-54752ec720f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273384} {:answer/id #uuid "7e50cbd9-a49c-5495-b667-fa105a197df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273468} {:answer/id #uuid "cae87b05-0578-5e12-b78c-39423534de2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e50cbd9-a49c-5495-b667-fa105a197df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273468} :answer-field/id #uuid "9725aa84-a0a7-5f0e-96c0-136120bcc8ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0a8af66-eea1-5639-ab77-2bc1523780a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149748" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-2, & x\\le 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=4$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=4,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=4,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=4,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9e8008-048e-5d52-af32-ca8c30b1b34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272665} {:answer/id #uuid "ab920b6e-5aad-535d-80b7-194b6cbe5ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273868} {:answer/id #uuid "da72c52a-a470-53be-a5e3-a28564f510be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274296} {:answer/id #uuid "ec99f1e0-fc55-55ce-a5ad-c6c0246e4283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274476} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b629aa-e668-5735-bf87-c9e28b80e3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab920b6e-5aad-535d-80b7-194b6cbe5ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273868} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94fcde2-7a90-5f43-b9d9-5ca2e2cb0449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc9788c2-4a6c-53d6-898a-18877d069efc" :question/math-academy-id "q-152925" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-x}, & x\\le -1 \\\\ x+1, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3-(-1)}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\sqrt{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15021175-4a1a-5a9f-8136-d7bc7497c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272576} {:answer/id #uuid "487ca92a-3acf-55d7-85da-b1c19c648b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273005} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9ba917-c2d9-5909-bb4b-e0359c38a146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273031} {:answer/id #uuid "53fcc9c3-6907-5f83-8eac-a58c2d61aa30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273103} {:answer/id #uuid "6234a335-fac5-5204-8a4f-d91e0c834031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15021175-4a1a-5a9f-8136-d7bc7497c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272576} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2177fe-6b49-5a9d-9447-4b016a086300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "238a8f80-b240-55d1-a1f3-be7bf8e77e18" :question/math-academy-id "q-156624" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=2y-1$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x=2y-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-1 \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-1 \\\\ (0) & =2(1)-1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dafb17-d53e-58e3-8f2c-867bbfbd5018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272475} {:answer/id #uuid "78b3c987-8686-55a2-9fb9-32e3742b5cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273417} {:answer/id #uuid "af6bc78d-b4de-5c47-980a-a819110c10bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273904} {:answer/id #uuid "b4682617-6c44-5562-8d58-aef5888f6886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273951} {:answer/id #uuid "bf68df2a-a19d-5a66-977c-803603cd56aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf68df2a-a19d-5a66-977c-803603cd56aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274042} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59d2e8c-e25b-594f-8642-a7de4539a7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "951ef63e-740e-55a7-b946-00aea8b9f2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-156727" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,2,3)$ and $(3,2,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since the second coordinate of any point on this line satisfies\n\n$$\ny=2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b5b039-f8d1-5ac0-84a2-bcd1b48b5132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273015} {:answer/id #uuid "4b401c7f-1af6-5c54-8b8e-0d0fa1d379c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273023} {:answer/id #uuid "68f0fe86-66a6-5d7f-a553-e10227b7a089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273294} {:answer/id #uuid "7677fc23-197f-5d23-aedf-ed8e18b43479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273396} {:answer/id #uuid "93643b07-3279-5a83-b854-54ef0257fec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7677fc23-197f-5d23-aedf-ed8e18b43479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273396} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e067aec-a6e4-5314-ab5a-93c2cad83e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36959921-7ebb-5f39-a723-592fbf72eec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-156728" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-5y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-5y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-5y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-5y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-5(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-5({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2k{a}_{1}-5k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-5y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6adcf6ee-01fc-563f-b9b6-d1b3c45b5e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273307} {:answer/id #uuid "8244231c-94b3-554a-a514-496b5826501b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273504} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fee998-0b79-5cf1-9270-9df51c0696d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274072} {:answer/id #uuid "c84b21f9-514b-56b8-8250-9200be09b088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274126} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ad941b-6a02-50a2-80b9-0f1a196e7ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6adcf6ee-01fc-563f-b9b6-d1b3c45b5e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273307} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a9989e-d052-5f69-af3d-b89ae23e841c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e786624-3795-5bd2-9044-40e13fec09b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-157162" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =-2[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d82b46-aff2-515f-9d9e-763c1d8d6d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272442} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae733d2-cc04-5192-bed0-23294bc49d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273728} {:answer/id #uuid "ac235c0f-3456-5dc9-bf15-4e714898bb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273872} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e7a6e4-13cc-528d-b440-977d47c6f5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274257} {:answer/id #uuid "f92b98ef-714d-56ae-84c7-20ba88faf358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae733d2-cc04-5192-bed0-23294bc49d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273728} :answer-field/id #uuid "3340ab30-5665-5c4b-ac26-0abb6f1cea04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97d72a17-2281-52f3-a3f9-d658f3c735d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157224" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =-2[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2+0 \\\\ 0+4+0 \\\\ 2-2+15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20033992-bd64-57d8-8dd4-bf7e04b02470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272670} {:answer/id #uuid "3eac531e-241f-5b68-9928-e92f5bdfff6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272923} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e42fdd-34c7-51a7-9c00-a2ceb9b43224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273832} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f3082-b5fa-59ad-8334-cdcbcfd0a82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274001} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a967b6-1436-5449-9aaf-9ed05176d4ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eac531e-241f-5b68-9928-e92f5bdfff6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272923} :answer-field/id #uuid "73031230-f674-5477-83c8-f4a76f429a85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b60b8aa-49bb-5ecd-ad04-b68a0c43f769" :question/math-academy-id "q-157225" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+0+0 \\\\ 5+0-9 \\\\ -2-20+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
